---
// src/pages/methodology.astro

export const prerender = true;

import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import {
  LABS_ID,
  ORGANIZATION_ID,
  SITE_ORIGIN,
  labsSchema,
} from "@/utils/site/schema";

const title = "Methodology - Sorted Tech";
const metaDescription =
  "Sorted Tech Methodology: learn how we test and research, following strict standards to give you 100% confidence in your tech buying decisions.";

const structuredData = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "WebPage",
    name: "Methodology",
    description: metaDescription,
    url: `${SITE_ORIGIN}/methodology`,
    mainEntity: {
      "@id": LABS_ID,
    },
    publisher: {
      "@id": ORGANIZATION_ID,
    },
  },
  labsSchema,
];
---

<BaseLayout {title} {metaDescription} {structuredData}>
  <section class="content-card rounded-none">
    <h1>Methodology</h1>
    <p class="mb-4 lg:mb-6">
      At Sorted Tech, all product evaluations are finalized by Sorted Tech <strong
        >L.A.B.S. (Laboratory for Assessment, Benchmarking & Studies)</strong
      >. This independent framework was developed to protect consumers from
      market noise by filtering every device through a rigorous multi-stage
      verification process.
    </p>

    <h2>1. Product Selection</h2>
    <p>We shortlist products using the following criteria:</p>
    <ul class="mb-6 list-disc pl-6 lg:mb-6">
      <li class="text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200">
        <strong>Relevance & Availability</strong>
        <p>
          Devices must have been released within the last 18 months and remain
          actively available for purchase in retail channels (no discontinued
          models).
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200">
        <strong>Software Longevity & Support</strong>
        <p>
          Devices must run Android 12 or later, and the manufacturer must
          provide a clear, publicly stated commitment to future software and
          security updates.
          <br />
          <span class="italic">
            Note: For newer brands without an established track record, this
            commitment is treated as a high-risk factor.
          </span>
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200">
        <strong>Product Integrity</strong>
        <p>
          The specifications printed on the box <strong>must</strong> match the actual
          hardware. We exclude any device where our technical audit finds discrepancies
          in advertised specifications. If a company is dishonest about what's inside
          the box, we won't review it.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2. What We Score</h2>
    <p>
      We score each device by evaluating its major components individually.
      Every component is assessed using measurable factors that affect everyday
      use. Here's what each category represents and what it's based on:
    </p>
    <ul class="mb-6 list-disc pl-6 lg:mb-6">
      <li class="text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200">
        <strong>Display Quality</strong>
        <p>
          Based on: panel quality, resolution, pixel density, refresh rate,
          brightness, dynamic range, and touch response.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200">
        <strong>Battery & Charging</strong>
        <p>Based on: overall endurance, power efficiency, and charging time.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200">
        <strong>Camera Quality</strong>
        <p>
          Based on: hardware elements such as resolution, aperture and lenses,
          as well as image quality in both daylight and low-light environments.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200">
        <strong>Hardware Performance</strong>
        <p>
          Based on: overall CPU (Single-Core and Multi-Core), GPU, RAM, and
          storage performance as measured by benchmarks.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200">
        <strong>Software Experience</strong>
        <p>
          Based on: software optimization, RAM management behavior, the presence
          of bloatware (OEM or third-party; type, size, background activity),
          the intrusiveness of services or ads (type, frequency, overall
          impact), as well as software and security updates (number of
          years/versions, frequency, consistency).
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>3. What We Do Not Score</h2>
    <p>We intentionally do not score the following:</p>
    <ul class="mb-6 list-disc pl-6 lg:mb-6">
      <li class="text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200">
        <strong>Design</strong>
        <p>
          Design is subjective, and different users have different preferences
          when it comes to weight, size, and overall aesthetics. As such, we
          avoid assigning scores to these elements. The IP rating, however, is
          an objective measure of a device's durability against dust and water,
          and since it already provides a clear indication of a device's build
          quality, we don't feel it's necessary to assign an additional score
          for it.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200">
        <strong>Network & Connectivity</strong>
        <p>
          Testing network and connectivity performance in a consistent,
          reproducible way is incredibly challenging. Factors like location and
          network conditions can drastically affect performance, and with most
          devices having similar configurations, results are often
          indistinguishable. Until a reliable, universal testing method is
          available, we do not rank these features. We will revisit this in the
          future if a robust benchmarking tool becomes available.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>4. How We Score</h2>
    <ul class="mb-6 list-disc pl-6 lg:mb-6">
      <li>
        We do not use composite benchmark scores such as AnTuTu. Instead, we
        prioritize component-level and sustained performance signals.
      </li>
      <li>
        We noticed that some important parts of a device like camera quality and
        long-term software experience are often judged through personal
        impressions, which adds unavoidable bias. We wanted to change that. We
        assess camera images using an in-house utility that applies multiple
        machine-learning techniques focused on noise, detail, exposure
        consistency, and overall perceptual quality.
      </li>
      <li>
        Evaluating software presents a similar challenge. Raw optimization is
        measurable, but long-term performance is much harder to capture. A
        device may feel fast when new but behave differently after extended use,
        and one review unit can't represent every user's experience. To reduce
        that gap, we leverage aspect-based sentiment analysis to refine our own
        testing, ensuring the end result reflects real-world behavior more
        accurately.
      </li>
      <li class="mb-4 lg:mb-6">
        Additionally, our scoring schema combines individual sub-component
        results and adjusts them for price, ensuring that the final ranking
        reflects a device's value within its price range.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>5. Price Range Classification</h2>
    <p class="mb-4 lg:mb-6">
      We determine a device's price range by researching its minimum and maximum
      prices using multiple price-comparison sources. Promotional or
      limited-time sale prices are excluded. We then calculate the average
      market price and assign the device to the appropriate price bracket.
    </p>

    <h2>6. Device Availability and Research</h2>
    <p>
      Due to limited funding, we do not purchase every device we review.
      Instead, we rely on crowdsourcing, reaching out to friends, family, and
      local distributors to borrow devices for testing. When in-house testing
      isn't possible, we conduct thorough component-specific research and rely
      on reputable third-party benchmark data, cross-checking it against trusted
      sources, including respected reviewers and publications.
    </p>
    <p class="mb-4 lg:mb-6">
      If we cannot find sufficient data on a device, especially if it is new or
      has not been widely available, we will not include it. We prioritize
      credible, verified sources and only add a device to our curations when we
      can confirm its performance through reliable, reproducible data.<br />
      You can find a list of all devices added or removed with each update on the
      respective curated pages in the "Overview and Changelog" section.
    </p>

    <h2>7. Update Frequency</h2>
    <p class="mb-4 lg:mb-6">
      We review our curations every fortnight. If we identify a new device for
      assessment, it will be published in the next update cycle, provided it
      meets the criteria outlined above. Each curated page includes a changelog
      detailing all devices that have been added, updated, or removed with each
      release.
    </p>

    <h2>Dev Note</h2>
    <p>
      Our goal is simple: to provide you with clear, objective insights into
      tech, with transparency at the heart of everything we do. We want to
      empower curious minds to make informed decisions, whether you're a tech
      enthusiast or just looking for solid advice.
    </p>
    <p>
      While our process is always evolving, we are committed to improving with
      each review, adapting to new tools, devices, and methods as they become
      available. We're always learning and growing right alongside the tech. If
      you have any feedback or ideas, please feel free to share. We value
      constructive criticism more than praise.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
